//? Title: Continuous Flows: Field Traces, RK4 Paths and Numerical Likelihood
//? Subtitle: A shared time-conditioned field, deterministic paired probes and a preserved banana fit
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A density model based on an evolving coordinate system must track both where
points move and how their local volume changes. A continuous normalizing flow
uses a velocity field for the first calculation and its divergence for the
second. This native C++ study starts with an analytically solvable scalar field
and a two-coordinate probe example, then preserves the original eight-step RK4
banana experiment, four quarter trajectories, all 48 Adam writes, actual
Jacobians and all 1,024 inverse samples. Its fixed-final numerical test NLL is
2.994471788 nats per two-coordinate row, just below the fitting-only full
Gaussian's 2.994631823 and above the diagonal Gaussian's 2.993811360 and analytic
truth's 2.796480843. The report distinguishes integrated field trace from the
actual discrete RK4-map determinant and retains numerical inverse and
normalization residuals. This short two-dimensional fit does not establish a
repeatable likelihood gain across seeds or a full-image density benchmark.
)
\toc

> Evolve coordinates and their local volume together

[Chen, Rubanova, Bettencourt and Duvenaud, Neural Ordinary Differential Equations
(2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.07366) describes continuous-depth models and
continuous normalizing flows. Here the trajectory x(t) solves x'=f(x,t), from
t=0 to 1. If J(t)=dx(t)/dx(0), then J'=A J with A=df/dx, and the determinant
identity gives

$$ \frac{d}{dt}\log|\det J(t)|=\operatorname{tr}(J^{-1}AJ)
=\operatorname{tr}(A),\qquad I(0)=0. $$
\alttext(The continuous log volume changes at the trace of the velocity-field Jacobian, by the determinant derivative and cyclic invariance of trace.)

A smooth finite tanh field has unique trajectories over this finite interval.
The model integrates x'=f and I'=trace(A), then applies a learned positive
diagonal scale. With z the final scaled coordinates, its numerical likelihood
calculation is

$$ \widetilde{\log p_X}(x)=-\log(2\pi)-\tfrac12\|z\|^2+I(1)
+\sum_c\mathrm{log\_scale}_c. $$
\alttext(The numerical log density adds the integrated field trace and final diagonal-scale log determinant to Gaussian latent log density.)

The continuous identity does not make I(1) the exact log determinant of a
finite-step RK4 map. We compute both and display their differences. Likewise,
reverse RK4 integration approximates the inverse of the continuous flow; it is
not an algebraic inverse of the finite forward map. Training differentiates
through every unrolled RK4 operation using ordinary reverse mode. This source
uses no adaptive solver or constant-memory continuous adjoint.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cnf_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);
 dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=10;s.height=5;s.font_size=32;
 d.node("state",{-24,0},"Coordinates x\nStructural time t",s)
 .node("field",{-11,0},"One shared field\n3 -> 8 tanh -> 2",s)
 .node("trace",{2,0},"Velocity f(x,t)\nPaired field trace",s)
 .node("rk",{15,0},"8 fixed RK4 steps\n4 quarter states",s)
 .node("density",{28,0},"Global scale\nGaussian density",s)
 .edge("state","field").edge("field","trace").edge("trace","rk").edge("rk","density")
 .save_svg("build/cnf_method.svg");
}
```
